    ÀÄÄÄÄÄÄÄÄÄÄÄÄÄÄÄÄÄÄÄÄÄÄÄÄÄÄÄÄÄÄÄÄÄÄÄÄÄÄÄÄÄÄÄÄÄÄÄÄÄÄÄÄÄÄÄÄÄÄÄÄÄÄÄÄÄÄÄÄÙ


    FONTMAN is a VGA character font editor for modification of VGA screen
    fonts in text mode with 25/28/33/40/50 lines (80 columns only).

    A character font can be saved in a file and activated any time
    (on the command line or in batch files) with the VGA.COM utility.

    Character fonts remain active until the video mode is changed or reset
    by a program (or by MODE command).

    Note: ****************************************************************
    Screen character fonts have no influence on printer character fonts!
    **********************************************************************


    VGA character matrix
    --------------------
    In standard text mode the VGA screen has 400 scan lines with 720
    (horizontal) pixels. This results in a character matrix of 16 scan
    lines by 9 pixels for the standard screen of 25 lines by 80 columns.

    Actually only 8 pixels are used in the character matrix. The 9th
    column remains blank, except for characters with ASCII code 192 thru
    223 decimal (hex C0 thru DF), where the 9th column is copied from the
    8th column by the VGA card to support continuous horizontal lines.

    The FONTMAN editing matrix automatically shows the correct status
    of the 9th column.

    Note: If you use a LCD display with only 400 by 640 Pixels, the 9th
    column does not exist. That means there is no extra space between
    characters. Keep this in mind, if you make character fonts for LCD
    displays.


    Font formats
    ------------
    Using a character matrix of less than 16 scan lines automatically
    increases the number of text lines that fit on the screen.

    FONTMAN supports the following formats:

        scan lines     lines      file
        /character    /screen     size
        ---------------------------------
           16           25        4096
           14           28        3584
           12           33        3072
           10           40        2560
            8           50        2048

    The file size results from the number of scan lines (with 8 bits)
    multiplied by a total of 256 characters.


    Loading character fonts
    -----------------------
    FONTMAN offers loading character fonts either from a file or from the
    VGA card, which has 3 built-in fonts with 16, 14 and 8 scan lines.

    If you choose to load from a file, a directory window is displayed
    for selection (to change drive: just type the drive letter).

    The format of the character set is determined by the file size.


    Format conversion
    -----------------
    Before you start editing, FONTMAN offers a format conversion.
    For example, you can use the SANSMALL.F12 font (12 scan lines) to make
    a smaller SanSerif font with full 16 scan lines (25 text lines).

    "Conversion" means that the appropriate number of scan lines at the
    top and the bottom of the character matrix is removed or added.
    For the graphic characters (ASCII code 176 thru 223) and for "ôõ"
    the pattern is extended into the additional lines.


    Display mode
    ------------
    For character fonts with less than 16 scan lines FONTMAN offers two
    display modes (toggle with F1):

    >>  "True mode" with the number of text lines (28..50) set according
        to the character matrix.

    >>  Standard 25 lines mode for easier handling.

    For the 25 lines mode the character font is internally converted to
    16 scan lines. Editing starts in this mode.


    FONTMAN Handling
    ----------------
    You may use your mouse for prompts, selection and editing. The mouse
    is operated in keyboard emulation mode, that is: mouse movement
    corresponds to cursor keys, left button = ENTER, right button = ESC.

    Prompts with bar also allow typing the first character of an item.


    Editing
    -------
    To modify a character matrix, you have to select a character three
    times (usually all three the same). Each code can be entered as a
    character or selected by cursor/mouse:

    (1) "Character code"
        With this code the result of your editing will be stored

    (2) "initialize with:"
        This character is taken as initial pattern. For example you can
        use "a" to design a new "„".

    (3) "to compare with:"
        This character can be shown any time during editing (by TAB key)
        to compare it with the current character pattern.

    After a character has been modified, it is activated in the current
    font to reflect the result of your effort.


    Test file
    ---------
    Sometimes you may wish to see, what graphic patterns look like in
    combination with others or in multiple arrangements. FONTMAN offers
    to display a test field in the character selection window (by F2 key).

    You can use or make any ASCII text file, and pass it to FONTMAN as
    command parameter. Only the first 8 lines by 32 columns will be taken.
    Example:
              FONTMAN \util\div\TEST.PAT

    Be sure to select "true display mode" (F1) if fonts with less than
    16 scan lines are edited.


    Using VGA.COM
    -------------
    Once you have saved a character font, you can activate it any time
    using the VGA.COM utility. Depending on the file size (the scan lines
    of the character matrix) VGA.COM switches to 25,28,33,40 or 50 text
    lines.

    Command:  VGA FONT <file>

    If you omit the filename, the built-in VGA font (25 text lines) is
    activated.

    Note: see VGA.DOC or run VGA/? for complete set of functions

